---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const steps = [
  {
    title: 'Start with a page',
    text: 'In Astro, each file in src/pages becomes a route. A simple .astro file can render a full page immediately.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Use components for reuse',
    text: 'Astro components are HTML-first building blocks. They render to static HTML, which keeps the default output fast.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Add interactivity only where needed',
    text: 'When a button, form, or widget needs JavaScript, hydrate just that piece with a client island instead of the whole page.',
  },
];

const basics = [
  'Install with npm create astro@latest.',
  'Put pages in src/pages and shared layouts in src/layouts.',
  'Use components for headers, cards, and repeated sections.',
  'Keep JavaScript small: Astro ships zero client JS by default unless you add it.',
];
---

<Layout title="Astro Basics for Beginners">
  <main class="min-h-screen bg-slate-950 text-slate-50">
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16 sm:px-10 lg:px-12 lg:py-20">
      <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-6">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-cyan-300">Beginner guide</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
          How to build applications with Astro
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300 sm:text-xl">
          Astro is a web framework for content-driven sites and apps. The beginner-friendly idea is simple:
          build pages from files, compose reusable components, and add JavaScript only to the parts that truly need it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-[1.3fr_0.7fr]">
        <article class="rounded-3xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-6 shadow-2xl shadow-cyan-950/20 backdrop-blur">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white">The basic workflow</h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
            {steps.map((step, index) => (
              <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900/70 p-5">
                <p class="text-sm font-medium text-cyan-300">Step {index + 1}</p>
                <h3 class="mt-1 text-lg font-semibold text-white">{step.title}</h3>
                <p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-300">{step.text}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </article>

        <aside class="space-y-6 rounded-3xl border border-cyan-400/20 bg-cyan-400/10 p-6">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white">Quick basics</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-200">
            {basics.map((item) => (
              <li class="flex gap-3 leading-7">
                <span class="mt-2 h-2 w-2 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-cyan-300" />
                <span>{item}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
        <section class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900/80 p-6">
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">1. Pages</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-slate-300">
            Astro uses file-based routing. If you create <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5">src/pages/about.astro</code>,
            it becomes <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5">/about</code>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900/80 p-6">
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">2. Components</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-slate-300">
            Components are reusable .astro files. They are great for nav bars, cards, and layout pieces.
            Astro renders them to HTML by default.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900/80 p-6">
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">3. Interactivity</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-slate-300">
            Add JavaScript only when needed. For example, use a small script or a hydrated framework component
            for a search box, toggle, or live form.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <section class="mt-10 rounded-3xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white">A tiny starter example</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-950/80">
          <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-5 text-sm leading-7 text-cyan-100"><code>{`---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="My Astro App">
  <main>
    <h1>Hello, Astro!</h1>
    <p>Build the page with HTML and add JS only where needed.</p>
  </main>
</Layout>`}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-7 text-slate-300">
          That pattern is the heart of Astro: use a layout, write page content in HTML-like syntax, and keep the
          frontend lean unless a feature truly needs client-side behavior.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10 rounded-3xl border border-cyan-400/20 bg-cyan-400/10 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white">Best next step</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl leading-7 text-slate-200">
          After this, try building a homepage, an about page, and one interactive widget. That gives you enough
          Astro to ship a small site without overcomplicating the stack.
        </p>
      </section>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
